[38;2;124;124;124mThe question: Consider a consistent and axiomatizable extension T of PA. Is it [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mpossible to have a sentence φ such that T ⊢ Prov_T(φ) but T ⊬ φ?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWe have derivability conditions:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m1. Reflection: if T ⊢ σ, then T ⊢ Prov_T(σ)[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m2. Formal modus ponens: T ⊢ Prov_T(a) ∧ Prov_T(a→b) → Prov_T(b)[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFirst, note that the standard derivability conditions (Hilbert-Bernays-Löb [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mconditions) are:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- D1: If T ⊢ σ, then T ⊢ Prov_T(⌜σ⌝)[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- D2: T ⊢ Prov_T(⌜σ→τ⌝) → (Prov_T(⌜σ⌝) → Prov_T(⌜τ⌝))[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- D3: T ⊢ Prov_T(⌜σ⌝) → Prov_T(⌜Prov_T(⌜σ⌝)⌝)[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe problem gives us D1 (reflection) and a version of D2 (formal modus ponens). [0m[38;2;124;124;124mIt does NOT give us D3 (the formalization of D1 itself, i.e., "Prov_T(σ) → [0m
